  "headline": "Oil-driven inflation, not fear, has yields above 5% and the dollar near its highs",
  "body": [
    "The dashboard describes an oil-led inflation squeeze that markets are pricing through the rates channel rather than the fear channel. Brent is up 73% this year and the 10-year yield has climbed 93 basis points over the same stretch, while gold, the classic haven, is down 3.8% year to date and sits near the floor of its 52-week range. That is not a growth scare; it is a market expecting dearer money to meet an energy shock, with Gulf conflict risk as the engine.",
    "Growth is holding. Copper is up 16% this year and the copper-to-gold ratio has risen 5% over the month, the standard pro-growth signal. Bitcoin is up 8.5% over the month and buys 12% more gold than it did a month ago, which speaks to liquidity and risk appetite rather than hedging. Gold falling as yields rise is the tell: higher real yields are punishing a non-yielding asset, and the conflict premium is lodging in oil instead.",
    "The dollar index sits at 95% of its yearly range and USD/JPY is up 2.3% over the month, consistent with the US being priced for tighter policy and benefiting as an energy exporter. The curve is flattening too: the 10-year is up 52 basis points over three months against 33 for the 30-year, which points to a policy-tightening path rather than a fiscal term-premium blowout. The one tension is this week, when yields fell 14 basis points while Brent rose 2%; that reads as a brief relief in rates, and the three-month trend carries more weight.",
    "The news bears directly on the oil channel. MintPress News reports Israel preparing further strikes on Iran and Lebanon with Saudi airspace closed under missile threats, and characterizes Ansarallah as controlling the Bab al-Mandab strait; Drop Site News reports Ukraine resuming strikes on Russian refineries and an EU-Gulf summit postponed amid Saudi airport strikes. Much of this is priced, with Brent at 79% of its range and volatility above its own yearly median, but the week's 2% gain against such headlines is modest. An attack on Iran as MintPress describes it would be bullish for Brent, bearish for long Treasuries through inflation expectations, and supportive of the dollar as the haven and exporter currency.",
    "The rupee is the outlier. USD/INR is down 1.8% this week despite India's oil-import exposure, the one instrument running against the story; a Gulf escalation would be a clear headwind for it. USD/CAD near its highs despite oil above 100 shows dollar strength overriding Canada's export benefit, and that only reverses if the dollar fades."
